WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.270
SPEAKER 1: Hey, everyone.

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:07.470
So I'm going to introduce
Dr. Abigail Person who

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.890
is an associate
professor and co-director

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.670
of the neuroscience program
at the University of Colorado

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.740
School of Medicine.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.680
She's an expert in
understanding the computations

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.110
in the cerebellum and its role
in smooth and precise movement

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.530
Dr. Person has been awarded
the Boettcher Foundation

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.259
Webb-Waring Biomedical Research
Award and the Esther A.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:37.020
And Joseph Klingenstein Fund
Award in the Neurosciences.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.390
Please welcome her.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.620
ABIGAIL PERSON: All
right, well, it's

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.370
a delight to be here seeing
the heroes of the field

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.120
in every direction.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.270
And so I really want to thank
[INAUDIBLE] for the invitation.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:55.930
It means a lot.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:02.730
So with that, I heard that MIT
is not a place for compliments,

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:06.060
and so I'm going to start with
an image of Mount Harvard,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.150
not to create a cross-town
rivalry or anything

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.600
but just to introduce
you to my home state.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.340
I've been giving a
lot of Zoom talks,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.120
and since I don't go there, I
bring myself to other people,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.530
but this hasn't yet changed.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.928
So I present to
you Mount Harvard.

00:01:21.928 --> 00:01:23.970
Now, the reason I'm showing
you this isn't really

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.070
just because I'm from
Colorado but because if we

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.770
were to gaze up onto
one of these snowfields,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.370
we might see a
creature like this.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.110
This mountain goat is
in fact demonstrating

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.530
a kind of precise action
which really motivates many

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.700
of the questions in my lab.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.390
So you can see that it's really
reaching for its next foothold

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:46.590
here, not unlike this now famous
climber Alex Honnold, who's

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.100
reaching for his next
handhold on his certifiably

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.490
insane rope-less ascent of
El Capitan in California.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.300
So I hope you can appreciate
that the precision of movement

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.570
is a life or death scenario
for these creatures.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.970
One imprecise
movement could lead

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.880
to the death of these animals.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.449
And so we're very interested in
how the brain actually mediates

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:10.660
these kinds of behaviors.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.900
Now, it turns out that
Alex Honnold has actually

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.290
had his brain scanned, and
there's something wrong,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.480
evidently, with his amygdala.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.150
But I would argue that
there's absolutely nothing

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.850
wrong with his
cerebellum, and the reason

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.040
that I can make that
speculation is that we've

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.050
known for over 100
years, really starting

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.110
with the work of a British
physician named Gordon

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:36.300
Holmes who studied World War
I veterans who suffered fairly

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.880
localized cerebellar
lesions, to this man who

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.360
has a unilateral cerebellar
lesion, that the cerebellum is

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.970
critical for precise
reaching movements.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.000
So I'm going to
show you this movie.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.680
This man is going
to reach to his nose

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.097
with his unaffected
limb, and then he's

00:02:52.097 --> 00:02:54.180
going to reach to his nose
with his affected limb.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.210
And you'll easily see
the kind of motor deficit

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.800
that characterizes
cerebellar damage.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.200
So that's the good nose--

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.780
or that's the good arm,
and this is the bad arm.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.480
So with the good
arm, he precisely

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.460
slows to touch his nose.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.500
With the bad arm he oscillates
right around that end point.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.030
So these oscillations, which
I'll show you one more time,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:22.170
have resonance with all
kinds of control theory

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.305
that I think many engineers
are very familiar with,

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.000
with control systems.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.840
So the problem is that when
sensory feedback is delayed

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.140
relative to the output of the
system, that there's naturally

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.650
oscillations that emerge.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.460
So the way I often
describe this with reaching

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.640
movements is if you were to
merely use your visual system

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.750
to reach for a particular
glass of water or something,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.720
the visual information would
be way out of date by the time

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.630
your fast reach brought
your limb to that target,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.370
and you'd overshoot the target.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.990
Then you would overcorrect,
and you would overshoot.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:59.010
And so that's essentially
what people hypothesize

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.960
these oscillations
around end points

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.570
are in patients with
cerebellar damage.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:07.350
And so it suggests that the
cerebellum may be computing

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:11.280
some kind of anticipatory
control that sort of obviates

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.110
this problem in
the nervous system.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.520
This slow sensory
feedback problem.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.519
So this is kind of
the working framework

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.550
that we have in the lab
is that the cerebellum may

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.820
be generating some kind
of anticipatory control.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.920
And there's a lot
of work that tries

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.430
to map the sort of
nature of information

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.910
that enters the cerebellum
through the mossy fibers

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.220
into the cerebellum
that sort of takes

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.250
two flavors, either sensory
information or motor

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.390
information.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:45.240
This ends up providing a very
rich spatial temporal context

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.330
for every movement that's
represented in the granule cell

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:52.380
layer, which then projects
into the Purkinje cell layer.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.360
The Purkinje
neurons are the sort

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.550
of learning workhorses
of the cerebellum,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.290
and there are many theories
about what they compute.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.920
I'm going to skip
over all of the debate

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.870
and just call it
an internal model.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.720
It's some kind of representation
of the body that's

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.860
localized to whatever sort of
effector we're talking about.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.040
Those Purkinje neurons are
inhibitory projection neurons.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.250
They project down to
the output structures

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.560
of the cerebellum which
are the cerebellar nuclei.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.190
The cerebellar nuclei
then distribute the output

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.490
computation of the cerebellum
to many different areas,

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.520
and that is sort of modular
depending on what cerebellar

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.630
nucleus we're talking about
along this medial to lateral

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.940
dimension.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.750
So this is the theory that we
have that we get to work with.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.840
But when we started the lab, the
evidence for any kind of feed

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.790
forward control coming
out of the cerebellum

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.230
was very marginal.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.960
And so we wanted
to know where do

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.970
we look for evidence of feed
forward anticipatory control.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:58.390
Well, we really
relied on data sets

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.150
that had been generated over
the years investigating limb

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.770
movements in a
variety of creatures,

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.790
including cats,
monkeys, and mice.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.380
And several papers, including
work from Claude Getz's lab

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.470
as well as work from
Albert Chen's lab,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.380
identified a particular
output structure,

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.470
the anterior interposed
nucleus, as being

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.360
critical for limb movements.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.530
So they would use either
muscimol inactivation

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.320
or genetic lesions
of output neurons

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.320
and found that those kinds
of inactivation schemes

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.880
recapitulated the
phenomenon of dysmetria.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.480
And so this gave us a place
to look but by no means

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.830
really isolated an
anticipatory control signature

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.490
because these long term
inactivations don't distinguish

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.380
between problems
in motor planning

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.750
or identify control policies.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.350
And so that's really where
we began when we wanted

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.865
to interrogate this idea.

00:06:58.865 --> 00:07:00.490
So the first work
I'm going to show you

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:03.460
is all work from a
fantastic graduate student

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.140
in the lab, Matt Becker.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:10.540
He combined a real time, three
dimensional limb tracking

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.360
with electrophysiology
and optogenetics

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.310
to address this question of what
does the interposed nucleus do

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.560
to control reaching movements?

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.520
And so he used a
motion capture system

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.660
so that we could
essentially get real time

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.120
readout of movement kinematics.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.750
And we use a mouse model for
many of the obvious reasons.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.990
And it's nice because
these reaching movements

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.710
are fairly discrete, so we
can look at many of them mice.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.390
Will readily engage
in this task,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:47.320
and we had this place to look
for some kind of cerebellar

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.650
control.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.520
So the first observation
we made using

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.250
electrophysiological recordings
from the interposed nucleus

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:57.250
is that neurons in
the interposed nucleus

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.690
are modulated during reach.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.970
So you can see an
example neuron here

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:05.140
where here we've aligned neural
activity to the reach onset,

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.020
and you can see that
the cells are modulated.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:11.200
However, this modulation is
really rather sloppy from trial

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.030
to trial, as I think
you can appreciate.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:17.680
However, if we simply
align the neural data

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.590
to the end point
of reaches, we find

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.310
that the neural
activity is, in fact,

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.960
much more sharply regulated.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.150
And so this was
interesting to us

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.700
because as you might
recall from the video,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.710
it's movement
endpoints that seem

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:35.530
to be preferentially
disturbed in patients

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:37.150
with cerebellar damage.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.600
Indeed, we saw this kind
of end point modulation

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:43.750
in many neurons within
the interposed nucleus.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.750
By no means all of the neurons,
but many of these cells

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:52.210
showed this kind of
endpoint related activity

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.220
which you can see
sort of happens right

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.762
during this decelerated phase
of the reaching movement.

00:08:57.762 --> 00:08:59.470
And so the natural
question that we asked

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.000
is, is this activity
causal for limb control?

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.535
So to address this question,
we introduced channelrhodopsin

00:09:07.535 --> 00:09:11.080
to the output neurons
of the cerebellar nuclei

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.620
within the anterior
interposed nucleus,

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.700
implant an optical
fiber over [? ente, ?]

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.960
and then used closed loop
optogenetic stimulation just

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.200
during reach on
randomized 25% of reaches

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.420
and asked what's the
kinematic consequence

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.640
of this perturbation.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.260
So this is a short stimulus.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:32.080
You'll see it displayed
up in the top,

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.130
and it's illustrated
here as well.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:35.980
So here's a mouse.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.530
It gets stimulated,
ends up with the pellet.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:40.570
Sometimes they don't
get the pellet, however,

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:43.300
so let's look at many reaches.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:47.440
So this illustration
shows unstimulated reaches

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.470
in orange and stimulated
reaches in blue.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:54.430
The point of the 50 millisecond
stimulation stimulus train

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:58.150
is shown here, and then I pull
down stills from this movie

00:09:58.150 --> 00:09:59.173
down here.

00:09:59.173 --> 00:10:01.090
And so what you can see
is early in the reach,

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:03.310
these reaches are
indistinguishable,

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:04.360
as you would expect.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.670
But right after the
stimulation, you

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.190
can see that the stimulated
reaches are not as far out,

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.500
and they're not as
far up, and then

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.050
there's only a late
phase correction.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.270
And so if we looked
at these reaches

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.280
in sort of outward
velocity space,

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:24.190
we'd find that the outward
velocity slows rapidly

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:26.470
in response to stimulation.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.330
And the upshot
here is that these

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.940
reaches end up hypometric.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:34.510
So the mouse is not
reaching as far in response

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:36.530
to this stimulation.

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:38.920
So I am going to
skip a bunch of data,

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:42.040
but we can show that
modulating the strength

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:44.020
of optogenetic
stimulation modulates

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:46.760
the strength of the
kinematic effect,

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:49.960
and that we can deliver
this stimulation

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.910
at any point in
reach and always see

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:54.170
this reduction in
outward velocity.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:56.080
So the obvious
question, then, if we

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.480
want to interpret the
activity that we observed

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.180
in the interposed nucleus
as causal for deceleration,

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.880
is that we should be
able to reduce activity

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.980
in that structure and
see the opposite effect.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.150
And so we did this experiment
where we introduced arch

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.160
into the interposed nucleus.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.550
What we found is, indeed,
a consistent increase

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.320
in all nine animals
that we tested this

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:19.190
in in the outward velocity.

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:22.690
So here we're sort of under
some biomechanical constraints

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.760
of the sort of
length of the limb,

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:29.440
but we see this very consistent
increase in outward velocity

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.360
during the arch inactivation
of the nucleus and concomitant

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.900
overshooting of the
reach end point.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:41.200
So these data,
summarized, give this idea

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.470
that this activity in
the interposed nucleus

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:47.110
acts as some kind
of a velocity dial

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.250
to regulate limb
kinematics, but there

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:55.160
are a lot of potential
interpretations of these data.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:12:00.160
So for example, we know that
activity from the motor cortex

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.140
is critical for driving
these movements.

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:03.820
This is in mice.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.880
Beautiful work done by Adam
Hantman's group, where they've

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.190
inactivated motor
cortex during reach,

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:12.320
and that halts the
movement entirely.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.670
So what is this
structure, the cerebellum,

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:20.330
doing to these putative
commands from motor cortex?

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:24.650
So our data are consistent
with at least two models.

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:28.030
The first is that maybe this
activity from cerebellum

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:31.490
is acting like something like
a brake or a gain controller.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:35.800
So if you imagine some sort
of an unfettered command

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.260
from cortex
generating a movement

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:40.600
with a particular outward
and upward velocity,

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:44.450
and then we stimulate the
cerebellum, and that's reduced.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.150
So that's one possibility.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:48.250
An equally tenable
possibility is

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:50.500
that there's some kind of a
directional command that's

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:55.120
in the opposite direction that
is being added to this control

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.060
signal from motor cortex.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:01.600
So these two possibilities
are interesting because they

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:03.460
make distinguishable
hypotheses about what

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:07.250
should happen if we stimulate
on return of the movement.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:11.290
So if we stimulate on return and
we are activating or reducing

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:15.610
the gain of that command or
we're just breaking the limb,

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:19.240
then we should show that
the limb will slow down

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:22.090
in response to that
stimulation, Alternatively,

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:26.140
if we're introducing sort of
a return vector of command,

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.270
we should actually
speed the limb up

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.680
if we stimulate on return.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:32.920
So we performed this
experiment, and our data

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:37.270
are consistent with this model
because when we stimulate

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.340
in the negative
velocity domain, we

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:44.180
find that the limb actually goes
faster in the return direction.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.470
So these data support
the view that there

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:52.450
is some kind of a summation
of directional control vectors

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:59.060
that are mediating the limb
movements in this paradigm.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.770
So we wanted to return
to the neural data

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:06.850
and ask a little bit of a
deeper question about refinement

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.080
of movement by the cerebellum.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:14.710
So we sorted reaches
by the firing rates

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:17.230
that were issued by
the interposed nucleus

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:22.750
during a particular reach and
separated those into deciles,

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:24.380
which are color-coded here.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.760
So the reaches that had
the highest firing rate,

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.950
not surprisingly what
I've already shown you,

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:33.910
decelerated the fastest, and
those with the lowest firing

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.980
rates decelerated the slowest.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:40.510
And this is also consistent
with other linear correlation

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.010
analyzes that we did.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:45.820
But why should we expect
trial-by-trial variability

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:48.530
in the firing rates
to happen at all?

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:51.970
So to address this,
we went back in time

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.900
from the epoch that we're
analyzing for firing rates here

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:59.680
and looked at the peak
velocity of the reaches.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:03.460
And we found that reaches
that decelerated the fastest

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.440
and had the highest
firing rates also

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.440
had the highest peak
velocities, and reaches

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:13.450
that decelerated the slowest
and had the lowest firing

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:17.020
rates also had the
lowest peak velocities.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.690
So this gets a little
bit wobbly, so--

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:24.670
but the upshot is if that-- is
that the end points, regardless

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:28.640
of this initial kinematic
variability, were stable.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.250
So if the animal reached slowly,
it would decelerate slowly,

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.060
and it would end on target.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.470
And if it reached very fast, it
would decelerate very quickly

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.130
and end on target.

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:41.980
And so the model
that we proposed

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:44.560
is that this activity
that's regulated

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:48.130
on a reach-by-reach reach
basis in the interposed nucleus

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:52.720
is accommodating a deceleration
that allows the limb

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.770
to end on target.

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:57.460
And we can illustrate
that if you did not

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:01.450
have this kind of scaled
activity from the interpose,

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:04.120
that reaches that
started out very slow

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.880
and had an average
rate of deceleration

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:08.620
would end hypometric.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.110
So they would slow down
essentially too fast

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:14.230
for a particular end point.

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:19.600
Conversely, reaches that
start out very fast and do not

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.330
decelerate fast
enough would end up

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:26.050
hypermetric with the average
deceleration profile.

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:30.160
So by regulating this
late-phase kinematic feature,

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:35.090
the cerebellum may improve
the precision of movements.

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:37.720
So that's kind of the
model that we had going,

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:39.940
where we have this
kind of peak velocity

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:43.150
that scales activity in
the interposed nucleus

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:47.450
to regulate the decelerative
phase of the reaching movement.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:51.250
And so the next question
that we wanted to address

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.710
is how this computation
might actually be made.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:59.260
And so to address this, we
made the very not controversial

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.930
decision to look at the
Purkinje neurons which

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:07.240
are, of course, the major driver
to the interposed nucleus.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.400
And so another very
talented student

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:12.250
in the lab, Dylan
Calame, ended up

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:16.839
targeting his neural recordings
to a part of the cerebellar

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:20.920
cortex that had previously been
identified by Tom Otis's lab

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:24.400
as controlling or
at least influencing

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:26.960
forelimb movements in mice.

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:30.850
And so, indeed, when he
recorded in this region,

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:35.140
he found many Purkinje neurons
that modulate their activity

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:36.710
around the time of reach.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:40.540
And so he could look at entire
populations of these cells

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:44.735
and find that there were cells
that increase their firing

00:17:44.735 --> 00:17:46.360
rate around the time
of reach and cells

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:48.760
that decrease their firing
rate around the reach.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:51.340
I should say that these are
all high firing rate cells.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:53.530
Many of them have
complex spikes,

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:58.100
and that's how we're identifying
them as Purkinje cells.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:02.200
So if we look at population
PSTHs of these two populations,

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.990
these increasers and
decreasers, if you will,

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:10.300
we find that they're practically
mirror images of the two.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:12.070
And for a very
long time, we were

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.800
spending a lot of
time doing all kinds

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.930
of sophisticated
regression techniques

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.240
to try to understand what
are these individual neurons

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:19.960
encoding?

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.360
What are the kinematic
features of their code?

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.500
And I can bore you
with bar graphs that

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:31.510
are 18 parameters
long, and so I was.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.970
We were just boring ourselves
with all of this data.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:35.560
And Reza Shadmehr
came and visited,

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:40.750
and he has been at the forefront
of thinking about populations

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:43.030
of Purkinje neurons
sort of conspiring

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:46.840
to generate comprehensive
codes at the population level.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:49.330
And he said, well, why don't
you just see what they're all

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.590
doing if you mix them up?

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:52.450
And so we did.

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:56.050
We combined all of these
neural recordings together,

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:58.540
and what we found was really
remarkable, given what

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:00.970
we had observed in the
interposed nucleus, which

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:03.010
to remind you, was
a linear scaling

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.490
of the increase in activity with
the magnitude of the decrease,

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:10.340
and it's scaled
with peak velocity.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.730
So what we observed in the
Purkinje neurons, which

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:15.520
are here sorted
by peak velocity,

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:20.200
is that there was a net
decrease in the activity

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:22.930
of the population
of Purkinje neurons

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:28.610
that also scaled in magnitude
relative to peak velocity.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.610
So the largest
pauses were happening

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:35.650
with the very fastest reaches,
and it sort of scales linearly.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:41.750
So if we match that magnitude
of a pause, if you will,

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:45.820
on to the scaled activity
of the interposed nucleus,

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:52.360
it stands to reason that
this disinhibition may

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:56.020
sort of regulate the amplitude
of the activity that's coming

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:59.000
out of the interposed nucleus.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.990
So that was a lot, but I hope
that you followed all that.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:06.190
So I showed you now what
Purkinje neurons are doing,

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:09.520
but I started this section
with this question of how

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:12.080
this computation might be made.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:15.220
And so I need to
take a little detour

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:18.880
into teaching you a little bit
more about cerebellar theory

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:19.948
in order to get here.

00:20:19.948 --> 00:20:21.490
And before I do
that, I want to point

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:26.090
at two temporal relationships
that emerge from these data.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:31.180
The first is that the max
outward acceleration peak

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:34.150
velocity, those are
parameters in kinematic space

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:38.640
that happened
before deceleration.

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:42.750
It's kind of obvious
that the reach kinematics

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.130
have a bell-shaped profile.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:48.330
So they accelerate then
decelerate to end point.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:51.210
But also, in patients
with cerebellar damage,

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:55.320
the timing of agonist
and [? braking ?]

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:58.980
antagonist muscles that's
seen in healthy patients

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.730
is delayed in
cerebellar patients.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:07.710
So there's some kind of this
and then that relationship

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.470
that emerges in
coordinated movements.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:14.430
And so we hypothesized
that this peak velocity

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:18.420
is serving as something
of a contextual cue

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:21.630
to the cerebellum
to then regulate

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:25.660
the amplitude of deceleration
by the structure.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:28.620
And so to expand on
this idea that there's

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:32.700
an association between
a this and then that,

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:34.080
we're going to take a left turn.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:35.872
I'm going to remind
people about cerebellar

00:21:35.872 --> 00:21:37.372
associative learning,
and then we're

00:21:37.372 --> 00:21:38.880
going to get back to reaching.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.640
So many people in their sort
of introductory neuroscience

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:44.580
classes will learn about
delay eyelid conditioning

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.370
in cerebellum, at which
point they'll fall asleep,

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:49.140
and then they'll wake
up again when people

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:50.550
are talking about cognition.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:53.470
But I'm going to beg
for your attention here.

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:57.960
So in delay eyelid conditioning,
classic Pavlovian sorts

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:01.200
of pairings, there's a
neutral conditioned stimulus.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:02.730
In this case, it's a tone.

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:06.190
The tone is played for
several hundred milliseconds.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:08.580
And at the very end
of the tone, a puff

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:11.010
is delivered-- an air puff
is delivered to the eye.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.920
This is an aversive stimulus
that causes a reflexive eye

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:17.460
blink in naive animals.

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:20.980
After repeated pairings
of the tone and the puff,

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.960
the tone and the puff,
the tone and the puff,

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:26.070
the animal learns to close its
eyelid just before the puff is

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:27.670
about to come.

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:30.990
So you can see that there's
this learned component

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:35.010
to anticipating the
time of the air puff.

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.860
Now, one critical piece of
this associative learning

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.390
is that there's a
time component to it.

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:42.390
So the animal does
not close its eye

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:44.160
at the beginning of the tone.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.580
It closes its eyelid
right before the puff

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:49.030
is going to happen.

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:53.820
And so neuroscientists like
Mike Mock and Dean Bonomo

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:57.510
have thought deeply about how
it is that this timing can

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:58.350
be learned.

00:22:58.350 --> 00:23:01.260
I should also add
that decades of work

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:06.697
showed that this task
requires the cerebellum.

00:23:06.697 --> 00:23:08.280
So we think this is
actually happening

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.410
within the cerebellum,
but the basic upshot

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.290
is that the mossy fiber
inputs to the cerebellum

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:17.760
carry this tone activity
and that granule cells break

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:20.790
that invariant tone
activity up into what

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:22.320
is called a temporal basis set.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.660
So there's sort
of chunks of time

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:28.590
that are represented by small
populations of granule cells.

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:31.980
Each one of those
populations converges

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.830
onto a Purkinje neuron, which
under the naive condition,

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.600
isn't doing anything except
inhibiting the nuclei,

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:39.340
and so there's no behavior.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:42.870
So what happens in the
presence of a puff of air

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:47.280
is that there is activity that
is generated in what is called

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.620
the inferior olive
which is a teaching

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:52.950
signal to the cerebellum,
details alighted.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.890
The point is that when this
teaching signal happens,

00:23:55.890 --> 00:24:00.840
there is a concomitant change
in the strength of granule cell

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:03.510
inputs to Purkinje neurons but
only in the cells that were

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:05.610
active right before the puff.

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:10.140
So this is how time
might then be associated

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:11.550
with an aversive event.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.250
That reduction in
synaptic strength

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.500
leads to pauses
in Purkinje cells

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.620
and common concomitant
disinhibition of the cerebellar

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:23.890
nuclei, and then that leads
to the conditioned response.

00:24:23.890 --> 00:24:26.070
So we hypothesized
that this activity

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:28.140
that we see during
reaching movements

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:31.650
in the interposed nucleus is
akin to a conditioned response.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:35.448
That is, it's responding to
some sort of initial cue.

00:24:35.448 --> 00:24:37.740
What does this have to do
with reach adaptation, right?

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:41.340
So I showed you
these velocity traces

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:44.440
of the reaching movement, this
bell shaped velocity profile.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.080
And so we would argue
that, let's say,

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:47.850
that this is going
to be a bad reach.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:49.650
It's going to
overshoot its target.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:54.720
So we have this initial
kinematic information

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.510
coming into the cerebellum
through the mossy fiber

00:24:57.510 --> 00:24:58.140
pathway.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.750
If it overshoots its
target, maybe there's

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.390
a complex spike, that
complex spike then

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:05.700
is going to go to
the Purkinje neurons,

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:08.790
change the synaptic
weights of the granule

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:12.300
cells that were active during
that sort of peak velocity

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:13.260
phase.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:19.110
Then, when this reach happens
again, the same people--

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.330
or the same peak
velocity may be reached,

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:25.020
but because of this
associative plasticity,

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:28.200
we think that there may be
an increase in the activity

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:33.840
of the nuclear neurons to
sort of drive reach slowing

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:36.930
to increase the rate of
deceleration and land

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:38.140
on target.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:39.870
So that's our
working hypothesis.

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:42.000
How on Earth do we
test any of this?

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:44.100
So one of the
tricks of the delay

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:47.760
eyelid conditioning
field is to substitute

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:51.630
the conditioned stimulus with
electrical or optogenetic

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:54.820
stimulation of mossy
fiber pathway directly.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.200
So this way you get around of
all the other stuff going on

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:01.890
in the brain, and you just
introduce the stimulus here.

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:06.000
And you can fictively associate
those things in rabbits.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:07.740
We don't have that
luxury because we're

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.660
looking at this
naturalistic movement,

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:13.090
but we borrowed a sort
of trick from them.

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:17.310
And so instead of
perturbing a reach

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:19.350
through some sort
of external means,

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:22.080
we're going to perturb a
reach through activation

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.760
of the mossy fiber
pathways themselves.

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.910
So we're essentially
superimposing

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:32.470
some kind of novel contextual
input into the circuit.

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:35.610
And so to do this, we
introduced channelrhodopsin

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:40.410
into the pontine nucleus of
the mouse, which expresses then

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:43.830
channelrhodopsin in the
mossy fibers very densely.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.830
And then implant
an optical fiber,

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:48.435
and then perform an
experiment that's

00:26:48.435 --> 00:26:50.310
conceptually similar to
the ones I showed you

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:53.010
in the interposed nucleus
where we stimulate in closed

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:55.500
loop with the movement.

00:26:55.500 --> 00:27:00.930
In this case, we're stimulating
slightly differently

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:03.480
because we're going to do
this in a block format.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:07.050
So rather than stimulating
in randomized ways,

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:10.710
we're going to stimulate such
that the animal can actually

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:14.880
hope to learn that every
reach gets this perturbation.

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.190
So also work from Adam
[? Hampton's ?] lab

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:19.440
had shown that stimulation
of the pontine fibers

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.670
can perturb the reach.

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:22.650
So we had good reason
to think that there

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:26.910
might be some kind of a
learnable contingency here.

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:30.750
So indeed, we found that
optogenetic activation

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:34.110
of the pontine afferents
caused overshoot

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:36.250
of the reaching movement.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:40.410
However, if we continuously
deliver that stimulus,

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:43.350
that effect of that
stimulus actually adapts.

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:47.220
So there's less of
a kinematic effect

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:50.700
late in the stimulus
block compared to early,

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:53.310
and when we turn
off the stimulus,

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:56.250
we see that there's
this opposite overshoot.

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:59.530
This is classic hallmark
of cerebellar adaptation.

00:27:59.530 --> 00:28:00.360
So this is cool.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:02.580
Now we've got an
adaptation paradigm

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:06.060
that we can use to explore
this idea, that re-adapts.

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:08.880
So we saw that in all
animals that we've tested.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.790
We know that activation
of these cells--

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:13.980
of these afferents
actually causes changes

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:16.200
in Purkinje cells,
but what happens

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.870
during that
within-session adaptation?

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:22.590
So to address this we've now
recorded from Purkinje neurons

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.710
during adaptation.

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:28.530
Skipping over some
details, neurons--

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:29.550
well, I don't have to.

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:32.040
Sometimes neurons are
activated by this stimulus

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.170
and other neurons are
inhibited sort of similar

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:38.190
to that kind of balance
of bursters and pausers

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:40.210
that I showed you about earlier.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:43.080
And so we grouped
cells, either they

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:46.770
were activated by stimulation
or inactivated by stimulation,

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:50.280
and watched how that
progressed through the course

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.690
of adaptation.

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:54.480
And what we found
is that neurons

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:58.500
that showed an activation to--

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.290
in response to optogenetic
stimulation or inactivation

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:07.260
in response slowly decay
back to essentially baseline.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:10.860
So if we look at
p-value maps, we

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.930
find that there's a
very high difference

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:17.160
between the stimulated and
control profiles of Purkinje

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:19.080
neurons early in the
stimulation block,

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:22.890
and that decays
with multiple trials

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:24.790
over the course of the block.

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:26.460
And so this is very cool.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:29.670
This is within-session
adaptation,

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:35.080
and it brings us to a question
of how is this happening?

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:38.850
So I'm not going to go into much
of the details of this model,

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:43.650
but we hypothesized sort of
consistent with the [? Mock ?]

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:46.980
and [? Bonomono ?] work
that there's a time varying

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:50.850
population of granule cells
active during the reach

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:54.300
and that our optogenetic
perturbation is driving

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:59.490
activity within this population,
which is then sort of decaying

00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:04.420
back to its target
zone during adaptation.

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:08.250
So it's interesting, if we look
at which synapses are actually

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.610
undergoing change during
this adaptation block,

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:15.840
we find that, of course,
the granule cells that we're

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:19.560
driving with our optogenetics,
they're changing,

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.490
so their weights are decayed.

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:24.540
But what's important to note
is that neurons that we are not

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:28.410
explicitly driving
are also changing

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.930
but in this way that's
temporally locked

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:32.470
to the stimulus.

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:39.100
And we think that this sort
of application of plasticity

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:41.920
to neurons that weren't
causing the problem

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:44.260
but were sort of
just coincidentally

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:46.960
active during the
perturbation is

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:50.530
essential for the
emergence of after effects

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:53.270
that are considered to be
the hallmark of learning.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:55.870
But we consider
them to be evidence

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.360
that there's a
misattribution of error

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:02.320
to the coincidentally
active cells.

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:05.620
So I'll end there
to summarize that we

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:10.675
think that we have at least the
beginnings of a model for feed

00:31:10.675 --> 00:31:14.020
forward control during
a volitional movement

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:15.190
in the cerebellum.

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:18.200
And that through
our experiments,

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:21.760
we have an idea that there
may be a conditioned response

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:24.220
in the nucleus that's
structuring precision

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:27.520
of endpoint that may be
learned by the population

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:31.870
activity in Purkinje cells and
that that is sort of temporally

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:35.110
produced through what
we think is happening

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:37.330
is a basis that is
forming in response

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:39.950
to naturalistic inputs,
which I didn't get into.

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:43.480
So I want to thank the
very wonderful people

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:46.780
I get to work with, Matt Becker
and Dylan Calame, most notably,

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:48.110
for this talk.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:50.818
And with that I'm happy
to take any questions.

00:31:50.818 --> 00:31:51.735
I realize I went fast.

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.040
SPEAKER 1: Thanks so
much for the great talk.

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.790
We have few minutes
for audience questions.

00:32:08.277 --> 00:32:09.360
SPEAKER 2: Thank you Abby.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.820
I really enjoyed that talk.

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:12.770
Any-- this is just
a detail question.

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:14.900
Any idea what mechanism
is responsible

00:32:14.900 --> 00:32:18.680
for sort of staggering
the delays of these cells?

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:21.360
ABIGAIL PERSON: Yeah, we have
a lot of ideas about that,

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:21.860
actually.

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:26.990
So I actually have a
figure from a manuscript

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:32.750
that is on bioRxiv now that is
explicitly about this topic.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:35.030
How does a base-- how
would a temporal basis set

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:38.010
form in the granule cell layer?

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:43.520
So this idea has
stumped the people

00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:46.130
that are studying the eyelid
conditioning because what

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.320
they have is an invariant
signal coming in.

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:50.510
And so they hypothesize
that there's

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:52.190
recurrent [? inhibition, ?]
and there's

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:55.152
all these kinds of specialized
mechanisms in the granule cell

00:32:55.152 --> 00:32:56.960
layer itself.

00:32:56.960 --> 00:33:00.200
So my student Jessie Gilmer,
whose work I didn't talk about,

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:01.880
he was building some models.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:04.802
And he showed this one figure,
and it had this basis set,

00:33:04.802 --> 00:33:06.510
and I was like, where
did that come from?

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:09.960
He's like, oh, I just put
in some naturalistic inputs.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.380
And so this was
surprising because I

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:14.210
don't think that
the people studying

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:17.360
the naturalistic movements had
really engaged with the timing

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:18.170
problem.

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:21.740
But it turns out if you
have time varying inputs

00:33:21.740 --> 00:33:26.600
in the mossy fibers, which we
model as Ornstein-Uhlenbeck

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:29.630
functions that have an
autocorrelation time of about

00:33:29.630 --> 00:33:33.680
100 milliseconds sort of similar
to the activity of mossy fiber

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:37.820
afferents, if you put
that structure of input

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:42.500
into a very simple model
of a summation circuit

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:45.860
in the granule cell layer where
each granule cell receives four

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:50.510
of these sort of noisy inputs,
that this basis set is just

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:51.560
an emergent property.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:54.110
It's actually very trivial
for the granule cell layer

00:33:54.110 --> 00:33:55.370
to generate this.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:58.130
It requires a
level of inhibition

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:01.070
that can float with
this, but that's

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:04.190
very consistent with the
inter-neuron network of Golgi

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:06.300
cells in the cerebellar cortex.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:11.343
So we think this is a highly
likely thing to happen.

00:34:11.343 --> 00:34:13.010
SPEAKER 3: Larry
Abbott and Nate Sawtell

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:15.300
have similar ideas in like--

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:17.310
ABIGAIL PERSON: In electric
fish, that's right.

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:22.469
So they have-- so in
the Kennedy paper from--

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:24.840
in Ann Kennedy's paper
from Nate Sawtell's lab,

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:28.650
they have this nice basis
set that they have observed

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:33.150
in fish granule cells, which
is probably the clearest basis

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:35.610
that anyone has in
a granule cell layer

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:37.590
in a cerebellar-like structure.

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:43.380
So with their-- at least
when I've talked to Nate,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:44.909
I think that they
think that there's

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:50.489
a-- because those electric organ
discharges are quite pulsatile,

00:34:50.489 --> 00:34:53.280
they think that there's
a special neuron called

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:56.250
a unipolar brush shell
that sort of extending

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:59.850
the duration of that pulsatile
length signal, which we think

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:04.320
is slightly different than
this kind of continuous motor

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:07.260
signal that might be--
motor and sensory sort

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:09.930
of reafference signal that would
be coming into the cerebellum

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:14.290
through the mossy fibers,
but it's a very good point.

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:18.750
So, right, we talked about it
with the post-docs and students

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:21.480
earlier today about
how there is really

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:26.760
a dearth of population
recordings in granule cells

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:29.610
at the millisecond time
scales that are really needed

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:30.900
to interrogate this idea.

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:33.970
So, yeah.

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:35.480
SPEAKER 1: So I have a question.

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:38.938
So we actually had a journal
club on a few of your papers,

00:35:38.938 --> 00:35:40.480
and one of the
questions that came up

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:44.680
was some of these
principles of computations

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:47.470
that you see for movement
in the cerebellum,

00:35:47.470 --> 00:35:49.960
how do you think
they may generalize

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:53.830
to sort of cognitive
computations that

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:56.320
happen in the cerebellum
but may not necessarily

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:57.510
be related to movement?

00:35:57.510 --> 00:35:59.510
ABIGAIL PERSON: Yeah,
it's such a good question.

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:01.360
It's really the
question of the hour,

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:06.760
and I think with [INAUDIBLE]
talk that-- the idea I think

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:10.720
would be that there's
sort of an expansion

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:14.890
of prefrontal cortices along
with the actual anterior--

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:19.390
actually, posterior and lateral
components of the cerebellum,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:23.660
and those have evolutionarily
grown together.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:27.620
And so I think it's
a very fair question.

00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:31.090
And I can only wildly
speculate, and it's probably

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:32.260
completely wrong.

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:35.860
But may-- we know that
there's some heterogeneity

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:41.080
in the circuit elements that
are seen in the granule cell

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:43.330
layer in different lobules.

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:46.120
So, for example, I brought
up these unipolar brush cells

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:52.480
that Nate Sawtell has
sort of speculated about.

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:56.410
Those are found very densely
in vestibular components,

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:58.990
and I don't know
whether they're seen

00:36:58.990 --> 00:37:04.780
in the more sort of
posterior lateral section--

00:37:04.780 --> 00:37:05.650
segments.

00:37:05.650 --> 00:37:09.070
But you might expect that time
constants of autocorrelation

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:10.990
are getting longer
and longer, and

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:13.900
that if the plasticity
mechanisms in the cerebellum

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:16.840
can keep up, I think there's
an eligibility trace question,

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:19.630
and maybe Jeff can sort
of enlighten us here.

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:25.600
But if the reverberation
throughout the brain

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:28.900
is robust enough to
consistently reactivate

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:33.010
the same kinds of cells
in the same cells, then

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:37.660
maybe there can be
essentially the same algorithm

00:37:37.660 --> 00:37:44.140
about error and
association of predictors

00:37:44.140 --> 00:37:48.040
to that that happens
in the motor regions

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:49.457
just at longer timescales.

00:37:49.457 --> 00:37:51.040
So I think it's all
about time scales.

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:53.620
It's all about can you
get a longer basis set?

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:56.860
Are the eligibility
traces for the plasticity

00:37:56.860 --> 00:38:01.473
that's required for change
sufficient for this?

00:38:01.473 --> 00:38:02.140
So I don't know.

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:04.960
That's kind of hand-waving,
probably not very satisfying,

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:08.650
but yeah, it's a great--
it's a very interesting set

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:10.570
of questions.

00:38:10.570 --> 00:38:11.780
SPEAKER 1: Thanks so much.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:16.040
Are there any more
questions from the audience?

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:18.110
I can ask one more sort
of high level question.

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:20.170
So at ICON, a lot
of us are thinking

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:25.840
about how to adapt the existing
theories in various domains

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:28.490
to capture more and
more complex behavior.

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:31.780
So in-- and it's becoming
increasingly possible

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:35.620
to measure more accurately
complex behavior and movement

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:38.380
like higher degree of
freedom movements in animals

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:40.390
and movement across
longer timescales

00:38:40.390 --> 00:38:41.960
in naturalistic environments.

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:45.640
So in what ways do you
think sort of these theories

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:48.040
and principles, say,
in the cerebellum,

00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:49.720
would need to be adapted?

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:51.910
Or would it just
be more of the same

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:54.100
when you're trying to
explain complex behavior?

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.350
Or would they need to
fundamentally change

00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:59.212
to explain sort of
more complex behavior?

00:38:59.212 --> 00:39:00.670
ABIGAIL PERSON:
Yeah, I don't know.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:02.087
I mean, I think
fundamentally, you

00:39:02.087 --> 00:39:04.010
have to have sharp questions.

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:05.440
Otherwise, you're lost, right?

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:10.690
So I think that we can talk
about more complex behavior,

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:14.410
but what about more complex
behavior are we talking about?

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:19.630
Is it the complexity itself
is it something else?

00:39:19.630 --> 00:39:24.550
Already, our behavior is
considered absurdly complex.

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:27.040
So different audiences
are like, oh, my God.

00:39:27.040 --> 00:39:29.620
Unconstrained reaching,
are you kidding me?

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:33.325
So I mean, people that study
motor control want a two--

00:39:33.325 --> 00:39:35.320
a planer, two-arm robot.

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:36.700
That's it.

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:39.280
So I think that we're making--

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:41.740
I think we have some
traction on this behavior,

00:39:41.740 --> 00:39:43.660
and it's already complex.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:45.820
And I think that
people can make headway

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:50.950
on much more complex tasks
if their questions are clear.

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.420
It's just a matter
of like what are

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:55.240
you-- what do you want to know?

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:56.790
SPEAKER 1: Thanks.

